<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    `main` gets the C runtime's declaration whether the image names it or not
    (`option elfmain on|off`), and an `envp` the body never reads is left out
    of the printed declaration (`option envpelide on|off`, both default on).

    The XML wrapper only provides the harness architecture; every pass loads
    an in-tree ELF through the CLI's own loader. `elfmainnamed_x86_64` is a
    gcc -O2 PIE whose `main` reads `argc` and `argv[1]` and never `envp`
    (source beside it); `elfmainnamed_stripped_x86_64` is the same image
    stripped, so the same `main` is reached through crt1's
    `_start` -> `__libc_start_main` decode instead of its own symbol.

    Pass 1 (`elfmain off`, unstripped) reproduces the defect: the symbol names
    the function `main`, but its declaration is whatever its body alone
    yields, an unsigned return and two anonymous slots, the second a struct
    whose fields are `argv[0]` and `argv[1]`.

    Passes 2 and 3 (the defaults, unstripped and stripped) print the same
    signature: `int main(int argc, char **argv)`, with `argv[1]` read as an
    element of the argument vector. The declaration analysis applies is the
    runtime's three-argument call, locked; only the printed line leaves the
    unread third argument out.

    Passes 4 and 5 (`envpelide off`) print all three arguments for both
    builds. Pass 6 is the ARM fixture `armlibcmain_le32`, whose `main`
    forwards its third argument register to `__printf_chk`: there `envp` is
    read, so the default prints it.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
